course: dictionary
lesson: 12435
title: Appearances can be deceptive
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some things are not what they seem. Deceptive is a word for this. Something deceptive gives a false idea, so you think it is one thing when it is really another.
explain: «The tall, thin glass looks like it holds lots of juice, but it is deceptive: it holds much less than it seems.» The clues are looks and but. Looks tells you what you think at first, and but tells you the truth is different.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «The glass gives a false idea of how much it holds.» That still makes sense, so deceptive means giving a false impression.
explain: Deceptive can also describe something meant to trick people. «Deceptive advertising» promises more than the product really gives. Deceptive is an adjective, so it describes a noun, and its forms are more deceptive and most deceptive.
fact: Deceptive means giving a false idea of what something is really like.
fact: «Appearances can be deceptive» means the way something looks may not show the truth.
fact: «Deceptively simple» means a problem looks easy but hides a trap.
fact: «Deceptive advertising» is meant to trick or mislead people into believing something false.
fact: Deceptive is an adjective: more deceptive, most deceptive.
exercise: «The tall, thin glass looked full, but it was deceptive.» What does deceptive mean here? | Full to the top with juice | Very clean and shiny | *Giving a false idea of how much it holds | Tall and thin like a pencil
exercise: «The sum looked deceptively simple, but it had a hidden trap.» What does deceptively simple mean? | It was very easy to see | *It looked easy but was harder than it seemed | It was simple and easy to do | It was very long and hard to read
exercise: Which phrase means that the way something looks may not show the truth? | Appearances are always true | Look before you leap | *Appearances can be deceptive | Deceptive advertising
test: Mia says, «The picture looks like a window, but it is only paint: it is deceptive.» Why is it deceptive? | It is made of glass | It is very bright and colourful | *It gives a false idea of what it really is | It is a real window
test: «The advert promised a free gift, but the gift cost money.» Why is this deceptive advertising? | It is an advert for a free gift that is true | *It is meant to trick people into believing something false | It has lots of bright colours | It is a very short advert
exam: Ella says, «The calm sea looks safe, but a strong current hides under it. It is deceptive.» What does Ella mean? | It is very salty and cold | *It looks calm and safe but hides danger | It is a sea that is always rough | It is calm all the way through
